<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Program A. `user_menu_category_assignments` — which MENU CATEGORIES a person may ring, per
      branch.

      ══ Why this table exists ══

      The owner asked for a counter till that can only sell drinks and a waiter who can sell
      everything, enforced by the SERVER rather than by a hidden button. The product could already
      CONFIGURE something that looked like it: V15 (pos_db) created `pos_terminal_categories` under
      a header saying "the categories a terminal offers", the admin screen has been writing rows
      into it since phase 28, and there are live rows in it right now. Nothing has ever read them.
      `PosTerminalCategoryRepository.findByTenantIdAndTerminalId` has exactly one caller —
      `PosTerminalServiceImpl.toDto`, which echoes the list back to the form that just submitted it.
      An administrator configures "Bar till offers Drinks only", saves, and the till shows the whole
      menu. That is the reported symptom and it is a config screen writing rows nobody reads.

      ══ Why per-USER and not per-terminal ══

      Because nothing signed says which terminal you are. There is no user→terminal table, no
      terminal claim on the token, and `orders.terminal_id` was deferred by V15's own header and
      never built. A terminal id can therefore only reach the server by the client asserting it, and
      kitchen-service already wrote the rule for that case: "a view scope a client can assert is not
      a view scope, it is a suggestion". A boundary keyed on a client-asserted terminal is one you
      defeat by editing a query parameter. Per-user is also what the owner actually asked for, so
      the security argument and the product argument agree.

      The terminal's category list is NOT dropped and NOT migrated. It survives as a DEVICE-LEVEL
      FILTER on the grid, which is the one thing a client-asserted id may safely do because it can
      only ever narrow what the person is already permitted. V15's own header draws exactly that
      distinction.

      ══ Why the category UUID and not a code, where 086 chose a code ══

      086 (`user_station_assignments`) stores the station CODE and argued it at length. Re-run
      against categories, two of its three reasons INVERT and the third is neutral:

        - "The code is already the routing key everywhere downstream." For categories the UUID is:
          `menu_items.category_id`, `pos_terminal_categories.category_id`,
          `menu_category_station_routes.category_id`, and MenuController's own `?categoryId=`
          parameter. A name would have to be resolved to an id before it could be used at all.
        - "A code cannot go stale under a rename." `menu_categories` HAS no code column — V1 gives
          it (id, tenant_id, name, description, sort_order, active) with UNIQUE (tenant_id, name) —
          and `MenuServiceImpl.updateCategory` sets the name unconditionally. Names here are
          renameable; a UUID is the thing that survives a rename.
        - "auth_db and pos_db are separate databases, so a UUID is a foreign key this codebase
          cannot enforce." Still true, and unchanged by the choice: a NAME stored here would be
          exactly as unenforceable. The consequence is the same self-correcting one 086 accepted —
          a stale id scopes the user to a category that no longer exists, which is visible on their
          own screen within one shift. Typo prevention belongs in the admin UI, which sources the
          list from pos-service.

      So the divergence from the neighbouring table is deliberate and reasoned, not accidental.

      ══ EMPTY MEANS EVERYTHING, AND THERE IS NO FLAG ══

      A user with no rows here may ring the WHOLE menu. That is V15's documented default and it
      survives here unchanged, because it is the state every existing user is in — the do-nothing
      state has to be the permissive one or this ships as an outage.

      There is deliberately NO `sells_all` boolean. V15 forbids one by name and gives the reason: a
      flag and the rows it summarises can disagree, and then no reader can tell which is right.
      `PermissionResolver.putStationScope` states the same rule one layer up and is sharper about
      the cost — if "unrestricted" had two spellings, the first downstream reader to treat an empty
      list as an empty allow-list would black out every till in the product, mid-service, on the day
      it deployed. Unrestricted is spelled by ABSENCE, at every layer: no rows here, no claim on the
      token, no restriction in the policy.

      ══ Scoping ══

      Unique on (tenant_id, user_id, branch_id, category_id): one row per category a user may ring,
      per branch, matching 040's convention for user_branch_roles and 086's for stations.

      Per-BRANCH, even though categories are tenant-scoped in pos_db and stations are branch-scoped.
      Not for symmetry alone: a person already holds different roles at different branches, and the
      resolver mints a token per branch assignment, so a tenant-wide scope could not express "runs
      the bar at one site and the whole floor at another" — which is the ordinary case for a
      multi-site operator and the reason this feature was asked for.

      Indexed on (tenant_id, user_id) because that is the lookup PermissionResolver makes on every
      login, refresh and branch switch. Without it this is a sequential scan on the hottest path in
      the product.

      ══ FORCE, not merely ENABLE ══

      ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY exempts the table's OWNER from its own policies. Postgres does that
      by design and it is why phase 17b spent a phase adding FORCE across pos_db after 16 tenants'
      rows were reachable from one connection. auth_db already runs FORCEd tables (041, 050, 056,
      058, 086). A new tenant-scoped table that is merely ENABLEd is that same defect, freshly
      planted.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-094-create-user-menu-category-assignments" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <createTable tableName="user_menu_category_assignments">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="tenant_id" type="UUID"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="user_id" type="UUID"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="branch_id" type="UUID"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <!-- pos_db menu_categories.id. Not declarable as a foreign key: separate database. -->
            <column name="category_id" type="UUID"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="is_active" type="BOOLEAN" defaultValueBoolean="true"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="NOW()"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="updated_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="NOW()"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="created_by" type="UUID"/>
            <column name="updated_by" type="UUID"/>
            <column name="deleted_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ"/>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="user_menu_category_assignments"
                             columnNames="tenant_id, user_id, branch_id, category_id"
                             constraintName="uk_user_menu_category_assignments"/>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="user_menu_category_assignments" baseColumnNames="user_id"
                                 referencedTableName="users" referencedColumnNames="id"
                                 constraintName="fk_user_menu_category_assignments_user"/>
        <createIndex tableName="user_menu_category_assignments" indexName="idx_user_menu_category_assignments_user">
            <column name="tenant_id"/>
            <column name="user_id"/>
        </createIndex>
        <rollback><dropTable tableName="user_menu_category_assignments"/></rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-094-rls-user-menu-category-assignments" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <comment>tenant_isolation, ENABLEd AND FORCEd — an ENABLE-only tenant table is the 17b defect</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            ALTER TABLE user_menu_category_assignments ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
            ALTER TABLE user_menu_category_assignments FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
            CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON user_menu_category_assignments
                USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true)::uuid);
            GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON user_menu_category_assignments TO auth_user;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DROP POLICY IF EXISTS tenant_isolation ON user_menu_category_assignments;
                ALTER TABLE user_menu_category_assignments NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
                ALTER TABLE user_menu_category_assignments DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
